Here is the C# code that utilizes the "Debug.Assert(.*?);" for the conditions mentioned in the comments:

```cs

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_ContainsContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

            bool New_Ret = arrList.Contains(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount == New_arrListCount); // Assuming the count remains the same
            Debug.Assert(Old_x == x); // Assuming the value of x remains the same
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX <= New_arrListIndexOfX); // Assuming index of x is same or more
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX <= New_arrListLastIndexOfX); // Assuming last index of x is same or more
            

	    // @@@ If the return value is false, index of x before is more than or equal to after
            Debug.Assert(!New_Ret || New_arrListIndexOfX >= Old_arrListIndexOfX);

        }
    }
}

```
Please note that this code has to be compiled to see if it runs without errors. The `Debug.Assert(.*?);` statement is not executed if the condition inside the bracket is not met - it acts as a failsafe mechanism. The comments marked with "@@@" suggest a condition that should hold true in a correct implementation.

The actual results of the assertions will depend on the specific logic of the actual ArrayList class. If you face any errors or unexpected behavior, it is better to check the actual implementation of the ArrayList class.
